": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 66, "title": "Spider-Man: Brand New Day", "year": "2026", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Alternate-universe stories", "note": "These MCU multiverse projects do not fit into the main timeline. For a first watch, use release order.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Loki — Season 1", "year": "2021", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "What If...? — Season 1", "year": "2021", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Loki — Season 2", "year": "2023", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "What If...? — Season 2", "year": "2023", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "What If...? — Season 3", "year": "2024", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Marvel Zombies", "year": "2025", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Deadpool & Wolverine", "year": "2024", "type": "movie", "notes": "Alternate universe"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "The Fantastic Four: First Steps", "year": "2025", "type": "movie", "notes": "Alternate universe"}]}]}, {"slug": "x-men", "title": "X-Men Watch Order", "franchise": "X-Men", "collection": "screen", "summary": "Untangle the prequels, time-travel reset, Wolverine films and Deadpool crossovers.", "intro": "X-Men has prequels, sequels, time travel and characters whose histories do not always match between films. That makes a single uninterrupted chronology look simpler than it really is. The useful approach is to understand the original history, the reset, and the later spin-offs.", "notes": "## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nRelease order is the strongest first-time option. If you want a smaller introduction, watch X-Men, X2, First Class and Days of Future Past, then expand from there. The branch explanation is useful for a rewatch, but no arrangement can remove contradictions written into the films themselves.\n\nSources: TechRadar’s release and timeline guide (https://www.techradar.com/how-to/how-to-watch-the-x-men-movies-in-order); X-Men film catalogue (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Men_(film_series)); JustWatch franchise guide (https://guides.justwatch.com/us/x-men-movies-in-order).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 7, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "X-Men: The Animated Series", "year": "1992", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "X-Men", "year": "2000", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "X-Men: Evolution", "year": "2000", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "X2", "year": "2003", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "X-Men: The Last Stand", "year": "2006", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "X-Men Origins: Wolverine", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Wolverine and the X-Men", "year": "2009", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "X-Men: First Class", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Marvel Anime: X-Men", "year": "2011", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "The Wolverine", "year": "2013", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "X-Men: Days of Future Past", "year": "2014", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Deadpool", "year": "2016", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 13, "title": "X-Men: Apocalypse", "year": "2016", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Logan", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Legion", "year": "2017", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; adjacent standalone continuity"}, {"rank": 16, "title": "The Gifted", "year": "2017", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 17, "title": "Deadpool 2", "year": "2018", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 18, "title": "Dark Phoenix", "year": "2019", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 19, "title": "The New Mutants", "year": "2020", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 20, "title": "Deadpool & Wolverine", "year": "2024", "type": "movie", "notes": "MCU crossover continuation — Film"}, {"rank": 21, "title": "X-Men ’97", "year": "2024", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; sequel to The Animated Series"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "First, the older history", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "First Class — its main story is set in 1962", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "X-Men Origins: Wolverine — its main Weapon X storyline follows later, despite an earlier historical opening", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "X-Men", "year": "2000", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "X2", "year": "2003", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "The Last Stand", "year": "2006", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "The Wolverine", "year": "2013", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Days of Future Past — begins from a future crisis and sends the story back to 1973, changing history", "year": "2014", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Then, the revised history", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Apocalypse — set in 1983", "year": "2016", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Dark Phoenix — set in 1992", "year": "2019", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Story order: animated X-Men continuity", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "X-Men: The Animated Series", "year": "1992", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "X-Men ’97", "year": "2024", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; direct continuation"}]}, {"heading": "Story order: separate TV continuities", "note": "\n\nThese series tell separate versions of the X-Men world; they do not slot into the Fox-film timeline.\n\nThe original trilogy should not simply be added after Dark Phoenix as though the reset changed nothing. First Class provides shared background, but even the prequels contain inconsistencies with the older films.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "X-Men: Evolution", "year": "2000", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Wolverine and the X-Men", "year": "2009", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Marvel Anime: X-Men", "year": "2011", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Legion", "year": "2017", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "The Gifted", "year": "2017", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Finally, the later character branches", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Deadpool (2016 • Film) → Deadpool 2 (2018 • Film) → Deadpool & Wolverine (2024 • Film) is Wade’s viewing sequence. The films play loosely with continuity and cross into multiverse storytelling", "year": "", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Logan", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": "is set in 2029. Treat it as a future conclusion to Wolverine’s story rather than proof that every previous film joins perfectly. Watch it before Deadpool 2 and Deadpool & Wolverine to avoid having its outcome spoiled"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "The New Mutants", "year": "2020", "type": "movie", "notes": "is largely self-contained. Its exact placement is too uncertain to assign a definitive calendar slot; watch it after becoming familiar with the franchise"}]}]}, {"slug": "transformers", "title": "Transformers Watch Order", "franchise": "Transformers", "collection": "screen", "summary": "Separate the Bay films, Bumblebee continuity and animated stories.", "intro": "Transformers has repeatedly reinvented its robots, their origins and their arrival on Earth. That is why putting every film into one historical list creates contradictions. Separate the different screen versions first, then follow the story within each one.", "notes": "## Where do the animated films belong?\n\nTransformers One presents its own version of the relationship between the characters who become Optimus Prime and Megatron. You can watch it independently; it is not a mandatory first chapter of the live-action films.\n\nThe Transformers: The Movie belongs to the original Generation 1 cartoon. For that continuity, watch the television series’ first two seasons, then the 1986 movie, then season 3. It does not bridge Bumblebee and the 2007 film.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nStart with Bumblebee for a character-focused introduction, followed by Rise of the Beasts. Choose Transformers (2007) if you want to begin the five-film action saga. Transformers One is another accessible starting point, provided you treat it as its own version of the mythology.\n\nSources: Transformers continuity and viewing guide (https://www.space.com/transformer-movies-in-order); Film release catalogue (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformers_(film_series)); Rise of the Beasts setting (https://ew.com/movies/transformers-rise-of-the-beasts-everything-we-know/).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 8, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "The Transformers / Generation 1", "year": "1984", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "The Transformers: The Movie", "year": "1986", "type": "tv", "notes": "animated, connected to the original television series — Animated Film"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Beast Wars: Transformers", "year": "1996", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Beast Machines", "year": "1999", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Transformers: Robots in Disguise", "year": "2000", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Transformers: Armada", "year": "2002", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Transformers: Energon", "year": "2004", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Transformers: Cybertron", "year": "2005", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Transformers", "year": "2007", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Transformers: Animated", "year": "2007", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Transformers: Prime", "year": "2010", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Transformers: Dark of the Moon", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Transformers: Age of Extinction", "year": "2014", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Transformers: Robots in Disguise", "year": "2015", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 16, "title": "Transformers: The Last Knight", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 17, "title": "Bumblebee", "year": "2018", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 18, "title": "Transformers: Cyberverse", "year": "2018", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 19, "title": "Transformers: War for Cybertron Trilogy", "year": "2020", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 20, "title": "Transformers: EarthSpark", "year": "2022", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 21, "title": "Transformers: Rise of the Beasts", "year": "2023", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 22, "title": "Transformers One", "year": "2024", "type": "movie", "notes": "animated origin story — Animated Film"}, {"rank": 23, "title": "Transformers: Cyberworld", "year": "2025", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order: Michael Bay’s five-film series", "note": "\n\nThis is also their release order. Ancient-history sequences and other flashbacks explain backstory; they do not move The Last Knight to the beginning of the marathon.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Transformers", "year": "2007", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Revenge of the Fallen", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Dark of the Moon", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Age of Extinction", "year": "2014", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "The Last Knight", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Story order: Bumblebee and Rise of the Beasts", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Bumblebee", "year": "2018", "type": "movie", "notes": "(2018 • Film) — set in 1987"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Rise of the Beasts", "year": "2023", "type": "movie", "notes": "(2023 • Film) — set in 1994"}]}, {"heading": "Story order: Generation 1 and Beast Era TV", "note": "\n\nThis classic animated branch continues through the Beast Era. The 1986 animated film belongs between the early and later Generation 1 seasons.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "The Transformers / Generation 1", "year": "1984", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; seasons 1–2"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "The Transformers: The Movie", "year": "1986", "type": "movie", "notes": "Animated Film"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "The Transformers / Generation 1", "year": "1986", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; later seasons"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Beast Wars: Transformers", "year": "1996", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Beast Machines", "year": "1999", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Story order: separate Transformers TV continuities", "note": "\n\nThese are separate reboots or continuity branches. Follow each branch independently.\n\nThese work as a newer continuity. Bumblebee began life as a prequel project, which helps explain conflicting descriptions online, but treating the finished films as a clean lead-in to all five Bay movies creates problems.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Armada → Energon → Cybertron", "year": "2002, 2004, 2005", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; Unicron Trilogy"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Transformers: Prime → Robots in Disguise (2015)", "year": "2010, 2015", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; Aligned continuity"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Transformers: Robots in Disguise", "year": "2000", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Transformers: Animated", "year": "2007", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Transformers: Cyberverse", "year": "2018", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Transformers: War for Cybertron Trilogy", "year": "2020", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Transformers: EarthSpark", "year": "2022", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Transformers: Cyberworld", "year": "2025", "type": "tv", "notes": "TV series; separate continuity"}]}]}, {"slug": "alien", "title": "Alien Watch Order", "franchise": "Alien", "collection": "screen", "summary": "Find Alien: Earth and Romulus in the timeline or begin with the original film.", "intro": "Alien’s release order begins with a crew encountering something terrifying and unfamiliar. Its story chronology begins earlier, with humanity searching for answers in space. Choosing between them changes how much you know before the original film’s mystery unfolds.", "notes": "## What about Alien vs. Predator?\n\nThe crossover films use a contemporary Earth setting, making them much earlier by calendar date. However, their history does not fit smoothly with every later Alien production. For a coherent main-series marathon, keep them as an optional two-film side route rather than the foundation of the timeline.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nWatch Alien and Aliens first. They establish the creature, Ripley and two very different approaches to suspense. Continue in release order, then use the chronological list for a second visit when you want to trace the prequels and intermediate stories.\n\nSources: Hulu’s Alien guide (https://www.hulu.com/guides/alien-movies-in-order); Rotten Tomatoes timeline (https://editorial.rottentomatoes.com/guide/alien-movies-in-order/).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 9, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "\n\nFor the crossover-inclusive release list, insert Alien vs. Predator (2004) and Aliens vs. Predator: Requiem (2007) between Resurrection and Prometheus.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Alien", "year": "1979", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Aliens", "year": "1986", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Alien 3", "year": "1992", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Alien Resurrection", "year": "1997", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Prometheus", "year": "2012", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Alien: Covenant", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Alien: Romulus", "year": "2024", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Alien: Earth", "year": "2025", "type": "tv", "notes": "television series, included as a companion — TV series"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order", "note": "\n\nThe timeline makes Romulus an interquel: its main story occurs between Alien and Aliens. Alien: Earth is earlier than the original film, but it is not a prerequisite for understanding Ripley’s introduction.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Prometheus", "year": "2012", "type": "movie", "notes": "its principal expedition takes place in 2093, after earlier opening scenes"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Alien: Covenant", "year": "2017", "type": "movie", "notes": "2104"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Alien: Earth", "year": "2025", "type": "tv", "notes": "2120"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Alien", "year": "1979", "type": "movie", "notes": "2122"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Alien: Romulus", "year": "2024", "type": "movie", "notes": "2142"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Aliens", "year": "1986", "type": "movie", "notes": "2179"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Alien 3", "year": "1992", "type": "movie", "notes": "follows Aliens"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Alien Resurrection", "year": "1997", "type": "movie", "notes": "roughly two centuries after Alien 3"}]}]}, {"slug": "harry-potter", "title": "Harry Potter Watch Order", "franchise": "Harry Potter", "collection": "screen", "summary": "Choose from the released films; the announced TV adaptation has not premiered yet.", "intro": "Harry Potter is one of the simplest major franchises to begin: the eight Harry-led films tell one developing story in release order. The decision becomes more interesting when you add Fantastic Beasts, which explores an earlier generation of the wizarding world.", "notes": "## Do you need to watch Fantastic Beasts first?\n\nNo. Harry’s introduction was designed to bring viewers into this world without prior knowledge. Fantastic Beasts adds history and familiar family connections, but its three released films do not provide a complete filmed account of every later event mentioned in Harry’s story.\n\nPhilosopher’s Stone and Sorcerer’s Stone are regional titles for the same first film. You do not need to watch both. The Cursed Child is a stage production, so it is not an extra movie in this list. A separate television adaptation should likewise be treated as a retelling, not an additional chapter between the existing films.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nWatch the eight Harry Potter films first, then the three Fantastic Beasts films. This lets the world open up through Harry’s perspective before you explore its earlier history. Choose full chronology for a rewatch when the major discoveries are already familiar.\n\nSources: Official Harry Potter film catalogue (https://www.harrypotter.com/discover/films); Official Fantastic Beasts page (https://www.harrypotter.com/discover/films/fantastic-beasts-and-where-to-find-them).\n\nEditorial note for Peppermoon: These are ten independent drafts, not published pages. Add your chosen article images before publishing. Recheck new releases when updating a guide, and retain the scope and continuity notes so readers know exactly what each list includes. Suggested slugs are proposals, not existing website links.", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 10, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Harry Potter and the Philosopher’s Stone / Sorcerer’s Stone", "year": "2001", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets", "year": "2002", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban", "year": "2004", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire", "year": "2005", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix", "year": "2007", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows — Part 1", "year": "2010", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows — Part 2", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them", "year": "2016", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Fantastic Beasts: The Crimes of Grindelwald", "year": "2018", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Fantastic Beasts: The Secrets of Dumbledore", "year": "2022", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "HBO’s Harry Potter series — announced for 2027; not released yet — TV series", "year": "", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them", "year": "2016", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "The Crimes of Grindelwald", "year": "2018", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "The Secrets of Dumbledore", "year": "2022", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Philosopher’s Stone / Sorcerer’s Stone", "year": "2001", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Chamber of Secrets", "year": "2002", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Prisoner of Azkaban", "year": "2004", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Goblet of Fire", "year": "2005", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Order of the Phoenix", "year": "2007", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Half-Blood Prince", "year": "2009", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Deathly Hallows — Part 1", "year": "2010", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Deathly Hallows — Part 2", "year": "2011", "type": "movie", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Story order: HBO TV adaptation (separate continuity)", "note": "\n\nThe announced HBO series adapts the books independently of the films. It has not premiered yet, so there is no watchable episode order.\n\nFantastic Beasts takes place decades before Harry starts at Hogwarts. Within each group, the story and release sequences match. The main Harry Potter films follow his school years and the conflict that eventually pulls him beyond the classroom.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "HBO’s Harry Potter series — announced for 2027; not released yet — TV series", "year": "", "type": "tv", "notes": ""}]}]}, {"slug": "assassins-creed", "title": "Assassin’s Creed Watch Order", "franchise": "Assassin’s Creed", "collection": "games", "summary": "Explore the historical adventures, release years and Black Flag remake.", "intro": "Assassin’s Creed jumps across centuries, but its present-day story moves forward through the games’ release order. Those are two different timelines. This guide lists the major story-led console and PC games, their release years, and where a remake retells an earlier chapter.", "notes": "## Which order should a new player choose?\n\nRelease order is best for following the modern-day plot and seeing the series evolve. Historical order is useful if you mainly want to tour the eras. For a shorter entry point, begin with Assassin’s Creed II or Origins; each introduces a substantial historical adventure without requiring every previous game.\n\nSources: Ubisoft’s series guide (https://news.ubisoft.com/en-us/article/6ceMaQ0MpYMPeN1mJxXlLF/how-to-play-the-assassins-creed-games-in-order); Ubisoft on Shadows (https://www.ubisoft.com/en-us/game/assassins-creed/shadows); Ubisoft on Black Flag Resynced (https://www.ubisoft.com/pt-br/game/assassins-creed/black-flag-resynced).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 11, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "\n\nThis is the main historical storyline. It leaves out mobile releases and smaller side stories such as Chronicles, Liberation and Freedom Cry. Those can be added to a later expanded edition.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Assassin’s Creed", "year": "2007", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Assassin’s Creed II", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Assassin’s Creed: Brotherhood", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Assassin’s Creed: Revelations", "year": "2011", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Assassin’s Creed III", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Assassin’s Creed IV: Black Flag", "year": "2013", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Rogue", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Unity", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Syndicate", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Origins", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Odyssey", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Valhalla", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Mirage", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Shadows", "year": "2025", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Black Flag Resynced", "year": "2026", "type": "game", "notes": "Game remake of Black Flag"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order by historical setting", "note": "\n\nChoose either Black Flag release. Resynced retells and expands the 2013 game; it is not another historical chapter to play after it. In Assassin’s Creed III, the early sequences overlap the end of Rogue, so this is a practical sequence rather than a perfect scene-by-scene splice.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Odyssey", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 431–422 BCE"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Origins", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 49–43 BCE"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Mirage", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Baghdad, 9th century"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Valhalla", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 872–878 CE"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Assassin’s Creed", "year": "2007", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1191"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Assassin’s Creed II", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1476–1499"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Assassin’s Creed: Brotherhood", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1499–1507"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Assassin’s Creed: Revelations", "year": "2011", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1511–1512"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Shadows", "year": "2025", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; begins in 1579"}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Assassin’s Creed IV: Black Flag (2013 • Game; 1715–1722) or Assassin’s Creed Black Flag Resynced (2026 • Remake Game; same core story)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Rogue", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1752–1760"}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Assassin’s Creed III", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1754–1783; overlaps Rogue"}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Unity", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1789–1794"}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Assassin’s Creed Syndicate", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1868"}]}]}, {"slug": "final-fantasy", "title": "Final Fantasy Watch Order", "franchise": "Final Fantasy", "collection": "games", "summary": "Find a starting point among standalone numbered worlds and connected subseries.", "intro": "Final Fantasy looks like one enormous numbered saga, but most numbered games tell separate stories in separate worlds. There is no franchise-wide chronology. The useful watch-order question is which connected subseries you want to follow.\n\nThis guide lists the sixteen mainline games by first release year, then gives story order for the best-known connected arcs. It does not attempt to list every mobile game, expansion, port or spin-off.", "notes": "## What about remakes?\n\nFinal Fantasy III (2006 • 3D Remake Game) retells the 1990 game. Final Fantasy VII Remake and Rebirth form a new multi-game interpretation of VII. Enhanced editions such as Intergrade and platform ports do not add another story chapter, so they are not separate entries in the main release list.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nPick one numbered title that appeals to you and begin there. If you want the best-known shared world, play Final Fantasy VII and its related projects. You do not need to complete Final Fantasy I before starting Final Fantasy XVI.\n\nSources: Square Enix on standalone mainline games (https://www.square-enix-games.com/en_US/news/final-fantasy-xvi-everything-you-need-to-know); Square Enix on the VII remake project (https://ffvii.square-enix-games.com/en-us/games/rebirth); Square Enix on Crisis Core (https://www.square-enix-games.com/en_US/news/which-final-fantasy-spinoff-should-i-play).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 12, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Mainline release order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Final Fantasy", "year": "1987", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Final Fantasy II", "year": "1988", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Final Fantasy III", "year": "1990", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Final Fantasy IV", "year": "1991", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Final Fantasy V", "year": "1992", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Final Fantasy VI", "year": "1994", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Final Fantasy VII", "year": "1997", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Final Fantasy VIII", "year": "1999", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Final Fantasy IX", "year": "2000", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Final Fantasy X", "year": "2001", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Final Fantasy XI", "year": "2002", "type": "game", "notes": "Online Game"}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Final Fantasy XII", "year": "2006", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Final Fantasy XIII", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Final Fantasy XIV", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": "Online Game; relaunched as A Realm Reborn in 2013"}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Final Fantasy XV", "year": "2016", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 16, "title": "Final Fantasy XVI", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Final Fantasy VII’s original continuity", "note": "\n\nFor a first play, consider starting with Final Fantasy VII before Crisis Core. The prequel assumes you know major revelations from the original. Advent Children released before Dirge of Cerberus, so viewers following original release order can watch the film first.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Crisis Core (2007 • Game) or Crisis Core Reunion (2022 • Remaster Game) — same prequel story", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Final Fantasy VII", "year": "1997", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Dirge of Cerberus", "year": "2006", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Advent Children", "year": "2005", "type": "movie", "notes": "Animated Film optional continuation"}]}, {"heading": "Final Fantasy VII remake project", "note": "\n\nThis project is a reimagining, not simply a graphics remake to insert into the original game’s continuity. Play Remake before Rebirth.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Final Fantasy VII Remake", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; expanded reimagining of the opening Midgar portion"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Final Fantasy VII Rebirth", "year": "2024", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; second released entry in the remake project"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "The third remake-project game has not been released as of October 2026", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Other direct sequel trilogies", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Final Fantasy X (2001 • Game) → Final Fantasy X-2 (2003 • Game sequel)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Final Fantasy XIII (2009 • Game) → Final Fantasy XIII-2 (2011 • Game sequel) → Lightning Returns: Final Fantasy XIII (2013 • Game sequel)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}]}, {"slug": "resident-evil", "title": "Resident Evil Watch Order", "franchise": "Resident Evil", "collection": "games", "summary": "Follow the outbreak saga with remakes identified as alternate versions.", "intro": "Resident Evil combines outbreak stories, recurring characters and several remakes. A remake retells an older game’s place in the story, so its modern release year does not move the events forward. This guide covers the main saga and the two Revelations side stories.", "notes": "## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nFor a modern first play, use the remakes of Resident Evil, 2, 3 and 4, then continue with 5, 6, 7, Village and Requiem. Add Code: Veronica and Revelations when you want the wider character story. If you prefer seeing how the series developed, use release order.\n\nSources: Capcom’s Resident Evil history and timeline (https://game.capcom.com/residentevil/en/re-history.html); Capcom’s Requiem portal (https://www.residentevil.com/requiem/en-asia/); Capcom 30th anniversary (https://game.capcom.com/residentevil/en/30th.html).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 13, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "\n\nThe 2019, 2020 and 2023 remakes are separate releases that retell their originals. Pick the original or remake for each chapter; there is no need to play both to understand the main plot. Code: Veronica remains a released 2000 game. Its announced remake is not included as a released game.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Resident Evil", "year": "1996", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Resident Evil 2", "year": "1998", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Resident Evil 3: Nemesis", "year": "1999", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Resident Evil Code: Veronica", "year": "2000", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Resident Evil 0", "year": "2002", "type": "game", "notes": "Game prequel"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Resident Evil", "year": "2002", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Resident Evil 4", "year": "2005", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Resident Evil 5", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Resident Evil: Revelations", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Resident Evil 6", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Resident Evil: Revelations 2", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Resident Evil 7: Biohazard", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Resident Evil 2", "year": "2019", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game"}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Resident Evil 3", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game"}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Resident Evil Village", "year": "2021", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 16, "title": "Resident Evil 4", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game"}, {"rank": 17, "title": "Resident Evil Requiem", "year": "2026", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Resident Evil 0", "year": "2002", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; July 1998"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Resident Evil (1996 • Game) or Resident Evil (2002 • Remake Game; July 1998)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Resident Evil 2 (1998 • Game) or Resident Evil 2 (2019 • Remake Game; September 1998)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Resident Evil 3: Nemesis (1999 • Game) or Resident Evil 3 (2020 • Remake Game; September–October 1998). Its events overlap Resident Evil 2; the order here avoids splitting games into sections", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Resident Evil Code: Veronica", "year": "2000", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; December 1998"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Resident Evil 4 (2005 • Game) or Resident Evil 4 (2023 • Remake Game; 2004)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Resident Evil: Revelations", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2005"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Resident Evil 5", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2009"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Resident Evil: Revelations 2", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; its story spans 2011 and 2013"}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Resident Evil 6", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2012–2013"}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Resident Evil 7: Biohazard", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2017"}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Resident Evil Village", "year": "2021", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2021"}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Shadows of Rose", "year": "2022", "type": "game", "notes": "Story DLC Game; 2037"}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Resident Evil Requiem", "year": "2026", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; later than Village, set in 2028"}]}]}, {"slug": "tomb-raider", "title": "Tomb Raider Watch Order", "franchise": "Tomb Raider", "collection": "games", "summary": "Choose a timeline: Classic, Legend or Survivor.", "intro": "Lara Croft has been rebooted more than once, so Tomb Raider does not have one continuous game timeline. The easiest way to avoid confusion is to follow one of its three main continuities: Classic, Legend or Survivor. Remasters preserve older adventures; remakes retell them.", "notes": "## What about Legacy of Atlantis?\n\nTomb Raider: Legacy of Atlantis is an announced reimagining of the 1996 debut, currently scheduled for 2027. It is not included in the released-game lists. When it launches, the guide should identify which continuity it follows before assigning it a story-order position.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nStart with the Survivor trilogy if you want a modern origin story and a clear three-game arc. Start with Legend, Anniversary and Underworld for the other connected trilogy. The classic games are best approached as their own older continuity.\n\nSources: Amazon Games announcement of the new games (https://www.amazongamestudios.com/en-gb/news/articles/tomb-raider-at-the-game-awards); PlayStation on the Legacy of Atlantis reimagining (https://blog.playstation.com/2025/12/11/tomb-raider-catalyst-and-tomb-raider-legacy-of-atlantis-coming-to-ps5/); Tomb Raider series history (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_Raider).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 14, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order: major story games", "note": "\n\nOptional co-op spin-offs include Lara Croft and the Guardian of Light (2010 • Game) and Lara Croft and the Temple of Osiris (2014 • Game). The Tomb Raider Remastered I–III collection (2024 • Remaster Collection) and IV–VI collection (2025 • Remaster Collection) bring older games to modern platforms; they are not new story chapters.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Tomb Raider", "year": "1996", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Tomb Raider II", "year": "1997", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Tomb Raider III", "year": "1998", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "The Last Revelation", "year": "1999", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Chronicles", "year": "2000", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "The Angel of Darkness", "year": "2003", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Legend", "year": "2006", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Anniversary", "year": "2007", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game of the 1996 adventure"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Underworld", "year": "2008", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Tomb Raider", "year": "2013", "type": "game", "notes": "Reboot Game"}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Rise of the Tomb Raider", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Shadow of the Tomb Raider", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Classic timeline", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Tomb Raider (1996 • Game) or Tomb Raider I–III Remastered (2024 • Remaster Collection; includes the same game)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Tomb Raider II", "year": "1997", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also in the 2024 collection"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Tomb Raider III", "year": "1998", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also in the 2024 collection"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "The Last Revelation", "year": "1999", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also in the 2025 collection"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Chronicles", "year": "2000", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also in the 2025 collection"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "The Angel of Darkness", "year": "2003", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also in the 2025 collection"}]}, {"heading": "Legend timeline", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Anniversary", "year": "2007", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game; retells Lara’s first adventure for this continuity"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Legend", "year": "2006", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Underworld", "year": "2008", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}, {"heading": "Survivor timeline", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Tomb Raider", "year": "2013", "type": "game", "notes": "Reboot Game"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Rise of the Tomb Raider", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Shadow of the Tomb Raider", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": ""}]}]}, {"slug": "metal-gear", "title": "Metal Gear Watch Order", "franchise": "Metal Gear", "collection": "games", "summary": "Follow Big Boss and Solid Snake by release or fictional story chronology.", "intro": "Metal Gear tells its saga out of sequence. The story begins with Big Boss during the Cold War, jumps to Solid Snake’s missions, and later returns to Big Boss’s rise. Release order preserves the reveals; chronological order follows the events in the games’ fictional history.\n\nThis guide covers the core story games and labels the 2025 Snake Eater remake as another way to play the same chapter.", "notes": "## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nPlay in release order if you are new. The series was built around surprises, and starting with the 1964 chapter can reveal context the original games intended to unfold later. If you already know the plot, chronological order makes Big Boss’s arc easier to follow.\n\nSources: Konami on Snake Eater Delta (https://www.konami.com/games/corporate/en/news/topics/20250306/); Konami’s Master Collection Vol. 2 (https://www.konami.com/games/us/en/topics/3378/); Metal Gear chronology guide (https://www.digitaltrends.com/gaming/all-metal-gear-games-in-order-by-release-date-and-chronologically/).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 15, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order", "note": "\n\nMetal Gear Solid: Master Collection Vol. 2 (2026 • Game Collection) makes Metal Gear Solid 4 and Peace Walker available in a new collection. It is a re-release package, not a new story chapter.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Metal Gear", "year": "1987", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Metal Gear 2: Solid Snake", "year": "1990", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Metal Gear Solid", "year": "1998", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 2: Sons of Liberty", "year": "2001", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 3: Snake Eater", "year": "2004", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 4: Guns of the Patriots", "year": "2008", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Metal Gear Solid: Peace Walker", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Metal Gear Rising: Revengeance", "year": "2013", "type": "game", "notes": "Action Game spin-off"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Metal Gear Solid V: Ground Zeroes", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Metal Gear Solid V: The Phantom Pain", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Metal Gear Solid Δ: Snake Eater", "year": "2025", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game of Snake Eater"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order", "note": "\n\nPortable Ops (2006 • Game) sits around 1970, but its status in the core continuity is debated. It is best treated as optional. The 2025 Delta remake takes the place of Snake Eater in a playthrough; playing both versions back-to-back is not needed for the story.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 3: Snake Eater (2004 • Game; 1964) or Metal Gear Solid Δ: Snake Eater (2025 • Remake Game; same story)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Metal Gear Solid: Peace Walker", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1974"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Metal Gear Solid V: Ground Zeroes", "year": "2014", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1975"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Metal Gear Solid V: The Phantom Pain", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1984"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Metal Gear", "year": "1987", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1995"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Metal Gear 2: Solid Snake", "year": "1990", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1999"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Metal Gear Solid", "year": "1998", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2005"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 2: Sons of Liberty", "year": "2001", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2007 and 2009"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Metal Gear Solid 4: Guns of the Patriots", "year": "2008", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2014"}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Metal Gear Rising: Revengeance", "year": "2013", "type": "game", "notes": "Action Game spin-off; 2018; optional epilogue"}]}]}, {"slug": "like-a-dragon", "title": "Like a Dragon / Yakuza Watch Order", "franchise": "Like a Dragon / Yakuza", "collection": "games", "summary": "Trace Kiryu’s saga, move into Ichiban’s games and choose the remakes.", "intro": "Like a Dragon, formerly known internationally as Yakuza, follows Kazuma Kiryu across decades before introducing Ichiban Kasuga as a new lead. The remakes let newcomers use a newer version of the first three adventures. They do not create extra chapters to play between the originals.", "notes": "## Should I play Yakuza 0 first?\n\nFor story chronology, yes. For a first-time player who wants to experience the series as it developed, Yakuza Kiwami is also a strong start, followed by Kiwami 2 and then the later games. Kiwami includes additions that assume familiarity with Yakuza 0, so either beginning works with that caveat.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nChoose the Kiwami remakes for the first three Kiryu chapters if you want updated presentation. Continue with Yakuza 4, 5 and 6, then move into Ichiban’s games. Judgment is a good optional side path in the same modern setting.\n\nSources: SEGA’s Like a Dragon game catalogue (https://ryu-ga-gotoku.com/asia_en/lineup/); SEGA on Kiwami 3 & Dark Ties (https://ryu-ga-gotoku.com/asia_en/info/detail/003594.html); SEGA studio history (https://ryu-ga-gotoku.com/studio/en/).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 16, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order: main saga and connected spin-offs", "note": "\n\nRelease years here refer to the games’ first release in Japan. Some titles arrived in other regions later. Yakuza 3, 4 and 5 also had remastered releases; those versions do not change their place in the story.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Yakuza", "year": "2005", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Yakuza 2", "year": "2006", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Yakuza 3", "year": "2009", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Yakuza 4", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Yakuza: Dead Souls", "year": "2011", "type": "game", "notes": "Optional alternate-story Game"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Yakuza 5", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Yakuza 0", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": "Game prequel"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Yakuza Kiwami", "year": "2016", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game of Yakuza"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Yakuza 6: The Song of Life", "year": "2016", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Yakuza Kiwami 2", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game of Yakuza 2"}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Judgment", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": "Detective Game spin-off"}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Yakuza: Like a Dragon", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; also called Like a Dragon in some regions"}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Lost Judgment", "year": "2021", "type": "game", "notes": "Detective Game sequel"}, {"rank": 14, "title": "Like a Dragon: Ishin!", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game of a 2014 historical spin-off"}, {"rank": 15, "title": "Like a Dragon Gaiden: The Man Who Erased His Name", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 16, "title": "Like a Dragon: Infinite Wealth", "year": "2024", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 17, "title": "Like a Dragon: Pirate Yakuza in Hawaii", "year": "2025", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 18, "title": "Yakuza Kiwami 3 & Dark Ties", "year": "2026", "type": "game", "notes": "Remake Game plus new side-story campaign"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Story order: Kiryu’s saga, then Ichiban’s", "note": "\n\nOptional historical stories: Like a Dragon: Ishin! (2023 • Remake Game; 1860s) is a separate period drama with familiar actors and names in a historical setting, not a chapter in Kiryu’s modern story. Dead Souls (2011 • Game) is an alternate zombie spin-off. Dark Ties (2026 • Side-story Game campaign) explores Yoshitaka Mine before the events of Yakuza 3; for context, play it after reaching the third chapter.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Yakuza 0", "year": "2015", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 1988"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Yakuza (2005 • Game; 1995 and 2005) or Yakuza Kiwami (2016 • Remake Game; same main story)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Yakuza 2 (2006 • Game; 2006) or Yakuza Kiwami 2 (2017 • Remake Game; same main story)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Yakuza 3 (2009 • Game; 2009) or Yakuza Kiwami 3 (2026 • Remake Game; same core chapter)", "year": "", "type": "game", "notes": ""}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Yakuza 4", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2010"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Yakuza 5", "year": "2012", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2012"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Yakuza 6: The Song of Life", "year": "2016", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2016"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Judgment", "year": "2018", "type": "game", "notes": "Detective Game; 2018; optional side story"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Yakuza: Like a Dragon", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2019–2020"}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Lost Judgment", "year": "2021", "type": "game", "notes": "Detective Game; 2021; optional side story"}, {"rank": 11, "title": "Like a Dragon Gaiden", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; overlaps Like a Dragon and bridges into Infinite Wealth"}, {"rank": 12, "title": "Like a Dragon: Infinite Wealth", "year": "2024", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; 2023–2024"}, {"rank": 13, "title": "Like a Dragon: Pirate Yakuza in Hawaii", "year": "2025", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; after Infinite Wealth"}]}]}, {"slug": "star-wars-games", "title": "Star Wars Games Watch Order", "franchise": "Star Wars Games", "collection": "games", "summary": "Separate the current canon adventures from Legends classics such as KOTOR.", "intro": "Star Wars games cover several continuities. Some belong to current screen canon; other celebrated adventures, including Knights of the Old Republic, are Legends stories. This guide focuses on narrative console and PC games rather than every LEGO, mobile, arcade and multiplayer release.", "notes": "## How does this connect with the Star Wars films and shows?\n\nThe games can enrich a screen marathon, but they are optional. Jedi: Fallen Order and Survivor sit during the Imperial era; Outlaws takes place between Episodes V and VI. KOTOR belongs to a separate Legends history, thousands of years before the films.\n\n## Peppermoon’s recommendation\n\nStart with Jedi: Fallen Order, then Jedi: Survivor for a focused, story-driven introduction. Outlaws is a separate adventure with a new lead. Choose KOTOR if you want a classic role-playing game and are comfortable with it being outside the current film continuity.\n\nSources: Star Wars game timeline overview (https://gametimeline.org/star-wars); Lucasfilm’s Fallen Order story context (https://time.com/5737129/respawn-entertainment-star-wars-fallen-order/); Outlaws setting (https://www.polygon.com/gaming/446047/star-wars-outlaws-timeline-syndicates-explainer); Jedi: Survivor sequel relationship (https://www.starwars.com/games-apps/star-wars-jedi-survivor).", "image_url": "", "image_alt": "", "status": "published", "display_order": 17, "release_sections": [{"heading": "Release order: selected story-led games", "note": "", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic", "year": "2003", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends RPG"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic II — The Sith Lords", "year": "2004", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends RPG"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Star Wars: Republic Commando", "year": "2005", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends Shooter"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Star Wars: The Force Unleashed", "year": "2008", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends Action-Adventure"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Star Wars: The Force Unleashed II", "year": "2010", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends Action-Adventure"}, {"rank": 6, "title": "Star Wars Battlefront II", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Canon Campaign Shooter"}, {"rank": 7, "title": "Star Wars Jedi: Fallen Order", "year": "2019", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Canon Action-Adventure"}, {"rank": 8, "title": "Star Wars: Squadrons", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Canon Flight Combat"}, {"rank": 9, "title": "Star Wars Jedi: Survivor", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Canon Action-Adventure"}, {"rank": 10, "title": "Star Wars Outlaws", "year": "2024", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Canon Open-World Adventure"}]}], "story_sections": [{"heading": "Legends, Old Republic era", "note": "\n\nThese games are not part of the current canon timeline. Republic Commando and The Force Unleashed games are also Legends and belong to separate story arcs; play them in release order.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Knights of the Old Republic", "year": "2003", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends RPG; about 4,000 years before the films"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Knights of the Old Republic II", "year": "2004", "type": "game", "notes": "Game; Legends RPG; follows the first game"}]}, {"heading": "Current canon, around the Empire and Rebellion", "note": "\n\nThis is an era guide, not a perfect day-by-day sequence. Battlefront II’s campaign jumps across several years, and Squadrons follows different pilots. The Jedi games form the most directly connected pair and should be played in order.", "items": [{"rank": 1, "title": "Jedi: Fallen Order", "year": "2019", "type": "game", "notes": "Canon Game; about 14 years before A New Hope"}, {"rank": 2, "title": "Jedi: Survivor", "year": "2023", "type": "game", "notes": "Canon Game; five years after Fallen Order"}, {"rank": 3, "title": "Outlaws", "year": "2024", "type": "game", "notes": "Canon Game; between The Empire Strikes Back and Return of the Jedi"}, {"rank": 4, "title": "Battlefront II campaign", "year": "2017", "type": "game", "notes": "Canon Game; begins around Return of the Jedi and spans into the sequel era"}, {"rank": 5, "title": "Squadrons", "year": "2020", "type": "game", "notes": "Canon Game; after Return of the Jedi"}]}]}]$peppermoon_guides_seed$::jsonb) as g(slug text,title text,franchise text,collection text,summary text,intro text,notes text,image_url text,image_alt text,status text,display_order integer,release_sections jsonb,story_sections jsonb)
